<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch10-page3-equilibria-replicator" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Equilibria of the Replicator System</title>

  <p>
    We have already seen some connections between equilibria of the
    replicator system, Nash equilibria, and evolutionarily stable states.
    This makes sense, since in each of these settings, an equilibrium is
    often a state that a population tends toward or gets stuck at. In this
    section, we look at some theorems that make these connections clearer!
  </p>
  <p>
    Before reading, let's recall two things:
  </p>
  <p>
    An equilibrium is <term>asymptotically stable</term> if every solution
    that starts close enough to it stays close to it and approaches it as
    <m>t \to \infty</m>. On a phase line, this means the arrows on both
    sides point toward it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The theorems also refer to the two conditions of Theorem 5.2, the
    Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria (see
    <xref ref="read-5-1-theorems" text="title"/>). For a population state
    <m>\sigma</m>, <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly
    when:
    <ol marker="(1)">
      <li>
        <p>
          every active strategy at <m>\sigma</m> earns the same payoff
          <m>\pi_1(s_i, \sigma)</m>, and
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          no inactive strategy earns more than the active strategies.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading: Section 10.6                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-10-6">
    <title>Section 10.6, Equilibria of the Replicator System</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 10.6</alert>, as follows:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Read the statements and proofs of Theorems 10.2 and 10.3, and
            the paragraph after the proof of Theorem 10.3 that interprets
            it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Read the statement of Theorem 10.4. You can skip Lemma 10.5 and
            the proof of Theorem 10.4.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Let's restate each theorem for reference and explore each one a bit
    further!
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Theorem 10.2                                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-10-2-restated">
    <title>Theorem 10.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p \in \Sigma</m>, and let <m>\sigma</m> be the corresponding
        population state. Then <m>p</m> is an equilibrium of the replicator
        system if and only if <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> satisfies condition
        (1) of the Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria (Theorem 5.2).
      </p>
      <p>
        In other words, if <m>p \in \Sigma_I</m> for some nonempty subset
        <m>I</m> of <m>\{1, \ldots, n\}</m>, then <m>p</m> is an equilibrium
        of the replicator system if and only if all
        <m>\pi_1(s_i, \sigma)</m> with <m>i \in I</m> are equal.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    So <alert>a population state is an equilibrium exactly when every active strategy earns the same payoff.</alert> As in condition (1) of the
    Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria, only the active strategies are compared.
  </p>

  <p>
    We saw this in Microsoft vs. Apple: the equilibrium
    <m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m> is exactly where Microsoft and Apple users earn
    the same payoff.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-thm-10-2-all-in-use">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose every strategy is active at <m>\sigma</m> (all
        <m>p_i \gt 0</m>). Which statement is true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\sigma</m> is an equilibrium if and only if
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Condition (2) of Theorem 5.2 is about inactive strategies,
            and here there are none. So a Nash equilibrium only needs
            condition (1), which is exactly what Theorem 10.2 requires for an
            equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\sigma</m> can be an equilibrium without
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> being a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. That can only happen if condition (2) fails, which
            needs an inactive strategy that does better. Here every strategy is active.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> can be a Nash equilibrium without
            <m>\sigma</m> being an equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. A Nash equilibrium satisfies condition (1), so by
            Theorem 10.2 it is always an equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Theorem 10.3                                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-10-3-restated">
    <title>Theorem 10.3</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p^* \in \Sigma_I</m> be an equilibrium of the replicator
        system, and let <m>\sigma^*</m> be the corresponding population
        state. Suppose <m>(\sigma^*, \sigma^*)</m> does not satisfy
        condition (2) of Theorem 5.2; that is, suppose there is an
        <m>i \notin I</m> such that
        <m>\pi_1(s_i, \sigma^*) \gt \pi_1(\sigma^*, \sigma^*)</m>. Let
        <m>J</m> be a subset of <m>\{1, \ldots, n\}</m> that includes both
        <m>i</m> and <m>I</m>. Then no solution <m>p(t)</m> of the
        replicator system that lies in <m>\Sigma_J</m> approaches
        <m>p^*</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    The idea is simpler than it looks! <alert>If an inactive strategy <m>s_i</m> would do better than the
    population average, the population can't settle at that state.</alert>
    In this case, <m>p^*</m> is an equilibrium, but it is an unstable one! If you introduce a few
    individuals using the better strategy <m>s_i</m>, their share grows, so the
    population moves away. (If that strategy stays inactive, the
    population can stay at <m>p^*</m>, since by Theorem 10.1(1) an inactive strategy never appears on its own.)
  </p>
  <p>
    Combined with Theorem 10.2, this says that <alert>a population can only
    settle at a Nash equilibrium.</alert> Theorem 10.2 handles condition
    (1), and Theorem 10.3 handles condition (2).
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-thm-10-3-hawks-doves">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Hawks and Doves, consider the all-dove state <m>p_1 = 0</m>.
        Hawks are inactive, but a hawk would earn <m>v</m> against the doves,
        while the doves earn only <m>\frac{v}{2} - t</m>. What does Theorem
        10.3 tell us?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The all-dove state is an equilibrium, but no solution with some
            hawks approaches it.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Since <m>v \gt \frac{v}{2} - t</m>, the inactive strategy
            <m>h</m> does better, so condition (2) fails. A few hawks invade,
            and the population moves away. That's why <m>p_1 = 0</m> was
            unstable on the phase line on the previous page.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The all-dove state is not an equilibrium.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The all-dove state is a pure state, so it is an
            equilibrium (Theorem 10.1(4)). Theorem 10.3 is about whether
            solutions approach it.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A population that starts with all doves will eventually have
            some hawks.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. An inactive strategy never appears on its own (Theorem
            10.1(1)), so an all-dove population stays all doves. Theorem 10.3
            is about populations that already have a few hawks.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Theorem 10.4                                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->


  <p>And last, we have Theorem 10.4!</p>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-10-4-restated">
    <title>Theorem 10.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p^* \in \Sigma</m>. Suppose the corresponding population
        state <m>\sigma^*</m> is evolutionarily stable. Then <m>p^*</m> is
        an asymptotically stable equilibrium of the replicator system.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    So <alert>every evolutionarily stable state is asymptotically
    stable.</alert> Being evolutionarily stable (Chapter 8) is a
    <em>static</em> condition: we look at a single, fixed state
    <m>\sigma^*</m> and check that any small group of invaders does worse
    than the rest of the population. Nothing changes over time. Being
    asymptotically stable is a <em>dynamic</em> condition: it is about how
    the population changes over time, returning to <m>\sigma^*</m> after
    small deviations. Theorem 10.4 says the static condition guarantees the
    dynamic one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This theorem <alert>only goes in one direction</alert>. An
    asymptotically stable equilibrium does not have to be an evolutionarily
    stable state. For games with two pure strategies, though, the two ideas
    match exactly in the four cases of Figure 10.3 (when <m>a \neq c</m> and
    <m>b \neq d</m>), as we saw in
    <xref ref="table-ch10-stable-vs-ess"/> on the previous page.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-thm-10-4-hawks-doves">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Hawks and Doves, the mixed state
        <m>\sigma^* = p_1^* h + (1 - p_1^*)d</m> with
        <m>p_1^* = \frac{v + 2t}{w + 2t}</m> is evolutionarily stable (case
        (4) of Theorem 8.5). What does Theorem 10.4 tell us?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>p_1^*</m> is an asymptotically stable equilibrium of the
            replicator system.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. This matches the phase line we found on the previous page,
            where the arrows on both sides point toward <m>p_1^*</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>p_1^*</m> is the only equilibrium of the replicator system.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. <m>p_1 = 0</m> and <m>p_1 = 1</m> are also equilibria
            (Theorem 10.1(4)). Theorem 10.4 is about stability, not about
            how many equilibria there are.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>p_1^*</m> is an equilibrium, but we can't say anything about
            its stability.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Since <m>\sigma^*</m> is evolutionarily stable,
            Theorem 10.4 tells us that <m>p_1^*</m> is asymptotically
            stable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>p_1^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium, but it is unstable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. It is a Nash equilibrium, but Theorem 10.4 says it is
            asymptotically stable, since <m>\sigma^*</m> is evolutionarily
            stable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Wrap-up                                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Let's finish by putting the theorems together.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ch10-wrapup-stability">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which of the following statements are true? <alert>Select all that apply.</alert>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="yes">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>\sigma</m> is an equilibrium of the replicator system, then
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            False. This is only guaranteed when every strategy is active.
            Theorem 10.2 gives condition (1) of Theorem 5.2, but not
            condition (2). For example, the all-dove state in Hawks and Doves
            is an equilibrium, but it is not a Nash equilibrium, since a hawk
            would do better.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Every evolutionarily stable state is asymptotically stable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            True. This is Theorem 10.4.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If a population state is not evolutionarily stable, then it is
            not asymptotically stable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            False. This doesn't follow from Theorem 10.4. The contrapositive
            of Theorem 10.4 is <q>if a state is not asymptotically stable,
            then it is not evolutionarily stable,</q> which is a different
            statement. For example, in the lizards game, <m>\sigma^*</m> is
            not evolutionarily stable, and Theorem 10.4 tells us nothing
            about its stability. (The analysis in Section 10.5 shows the
            population cycles around it, but that comes from a separate
            argument.)
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For a game with two pure strategies (with <m>a \neq c</m> and
            <m>b \neq d</m>), a population state is asymptotically stable
            exactly when it is evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            True. This is what we saw on the previous page: in all four
            cases, the stable equilibria are exactly the evolutionarily
            stable states.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Notice the difference between the first statement in this checkpoint and
    <xref ref="check-thm-10-2-all-in-use"/>. In
    <xref ref="check-thm-10-2-all-in-use"/>, every strategy was active, so
    there was nothing to check in condition (2) of Theorem 5.2! Here,
    <m>\sigma</m> could have inactive strategies.
  </p>
  <interactive xml:id="question-form-10-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=10.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
